1
00:07:31,199 --> 00:07:37,199
At long last Redmond Barry became a gentleman -- and that was his tragedy.

2
00:15:02,399 --> 00:15:08,399
Barry Lyndon is a drama, romance, war film directed by Stanley Kubrick.

3
00:22:33,599 --> 00:22:39,599
An Irish rogue uses his cunning and wit to work his way up the social classes of 18th century
England, transforming himself from the humble Redmond Barry into the noble Barry Lyndon.

4
00:30:04,799 --> 00:30:10,799
Barry Lyndon, played by Ryan O'Neal.

5
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:42,000
Lady Lyndon, played by Marisa Berenson.

6
00:45:07,199 --> 00:45:13,199
The Chevalier, played by Patrick Magee.

7
00:52:38,400 --> 00:52:44,400
Captain Potzdorf, played by Hardy Krüger.

8
01:00:09,599 --> 01:00:15,599
Lord Ludd, played by Steven Berkoff.

9
01:07:40,799 --> 01:07:46,799
Nora, played by Gay Hamilton.

10
01:15:12,000 --> 01:15:18,000
Barry Lyndon is a 1975 historical drama film written, directed, and produced by Stanley Kubrick,
based on the 1844 novel The Luck of Barry Lyndon by William Makepeace Thackeray.

11
01:22:43,199 --> 01:22:49,199
Narrated by Michael Hordern, and starring Ryan O'Neal, Marisa Berenson, Patrick Magee, Leonard Rossiter, and Hardy Krüger, the film recounts the early exploits and later unravelling of
an 18th-century Irish rogue and gold digger who marries a rich widow in order to attempt to climb the social ladder and assume her late husband's aristocratic position.

12
01:30:14,399 --> 01:30:20,399
Kubrick began production on Barry Lyndon after his 1971 film A Clockwork Orange.

13
01:37:45,599 --> 01:37:51,599
He had originally intended to direct a biopic on Napoleon, but lost his financing because of
the commercial failure of the similar 1970 Dino De Laurentiis–produced Waterloo.

14
01:45:16,800 --> 01:45:22,800
Kubrick eventually directed Barry Lyndon, set partially during the Seven
Years' War, utilising his research from the Napoleon project.

15
01:52:48,000 --> 01:52:54,000
Filming began in December 1973 and lasted roughly eight months,
taking place in England, Ireland, and Germany.

16
02:00:19,199 --> 02:00:25,199
The film's cinematography has been described as ground-breaking.

17
02:07:50,399 --> 02:07:56,399
Especially notable are the long double shots, usually ended with a slow backwards zoom, the scenes
shot entirely in candlelight, and the settings based on William Hogarth paintings.

18
02:15:21,599 --> 02:15:27,599
The exteriors were filmed on location in England, Ireland, and
Germany, with the interiors shot mainly in London.

19
02:22:52,799 --> 02:22:58,799
The production had problems related to logistics, weather, and politics
(Kubrick feared that he might be an IRA hostage target).

20
02:30:24,000 --> 02:30:30,000
Barry Lyndon won four Oscars at the 48th Academy Awards: Best Scoring: Original Song Score and Adaptation
or Scoring: Adaptation, Best Costume Design, Best Art Direction, and Best Cinematography.

21
02:37:55,199 --> 02:38:01,199
Although some critics took issue with the film's slow pace and restrained emotion, its reputation, like that of many of
Kubrick's works, has grown over time, and it is widely considered one of the greatest films of all time.

22
02:45:26,399 --> 02:45:32,399
In the 2022 Sight & Sound Greatest Films of All Time poll, Barry Lyndon
placed 12th in the directors' poll and 45th in the critics' poll.

23
02:52:57,600 --> 02:53:03,600
The film explores themes of based on novel or book,
gambling, desertion, fencing, palace.

24
03:00:28,799 --> 03:00:34,799
The tone is emotional.
